            "text": "{\n  \"relation\": \"MATCH\",\n  \"consensus_summary\": \"Gehenna symbolizes the final divine judgment and punishment of the wicked (hell). It is rooted in the Old Testament history of the Valley of Hinnom as a site of idolatrous child sacrifice and divine judgment, and is widely distinguished from the intermediate state of the dead (Hades/Sheol).\",\n  \"book_summary\": \"Gehenna pictures the destructive judgment of both body and soul, drawing upon the historical Valley of Hinnom's associations with child sacrifice and its renaming as the Valley of Slaughter. It is distinct from Sheol.\",\n  \"consensus_core_identification\": \"The final, post-judgment punishment of the wicked (hell).\",\n  \"book_core_identification\": \"The judgment that destroys both body and soul, pictured by the historical Valley of Hinnom.\",\n  \"core_relation\": \"EQUIVALENT\",\n  \"extension_relation\": \"SAME_CASES\",\n  \"boundary_disagreement\": \"None. Both identify Gehenna as the final eschatological judgment of the wicked, rooted in the Old Testament history of the Valley of Hinnom and distinct from Sheol/Hades.\",\n  \"material_contradiction\": \"None.\",\n  \"rationale\": \"The book and the consensus share the same core identification: Gehenna symbolizes the final judgment/destruction of body and soul, drawing from the literal Valley of Hinnom and its prophetic renaming as the Valley of Slaughter. Both explicitly differentiate it from Sheol.\",\n  \"citation_support\": \"STRONG\",\n  \"strongest_objection\": \"It could be argued that the book's emphasis on a 'destructive verdict' (quoting Matthew 10:28) leans toward the minority annihilationist view rather than the dominant eternal conscious torment view. However, because it relies on direct biblical phrasing without explicitly ruling out mainstream interpretations, it remains firmly within the consensus core.\",\n  \"confidence\": \"high\"\n}",
